    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the graphs of $x+4y=3$ and $y=\\frac{1}{x^2}$ intersect at exactly two points.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Exactly two points: $(-1,1)$ and $(2,\\frac14)$.\n\nSubstitution gives $(x+1)(x-2)^2=0$, with two distinct real solutions.",
          "worked": "At an intersection, $x\\ne0$. Substituting $y=\\frac1{x^2}$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}x+\\frac4{x^2}&=3\\\\x^3-3x^2+4&=0\\\\(x+1)(x^2-4x+4)&=0\\\\(x+1)(x-2)^2&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThe Factor Theorem gives the factor $x+1$ since $(-1)^3-3(-1)^2+4=0$. The distinct solutions are $x=-1$ and $x=2$, giving $y=1$ and $y=\\frac14$, respectively. Thus the graphs intersect at exactly $(-1,1)$ and $(2,\\frac14)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 49
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that the graphs of $2x+9y=7$ and $y=\\frac{1}{x^2}$ intersect at exactly three points.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Exactly three points: $(-1,1)$, $(\\frac32,\\frac49)$ and $(3,\\frac19)$.\n\nSubstitution gives $(x+1)(2x-3)(x-3)=0$, with three distinct real solutions.",
          "worked": "At an intersection, $x\\ne0$. Substituting $y=\\frac1{x^2}$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}2x+\\frac9{x^2}&=7\\\\2x^3-7x^2+9&=0\\\\(x+1)(2x^2-9x+9)&=0\\\\(x+1)(2x-3)(x-3)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThe Factor Theorem gives the factor $x+1$ since $2(-1)^3-7(-1)^2+9=0$. The distinct solutions are $x=-1$, $x=\\frac32$ and $x=3$. Substituting into $y=\\frac1{x^2}$ gives $y=1$, $y=\\frac49$ and $y=\\frac19$, respectively. These are exactly three intersection points.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 49
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q9-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Are there any lines that intersect the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x^2}$ at exactly one point?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Yes. Every vertical line $x=c$, where $c\\ne0$, intersects it once, at $(c,\\frac1{c^2})$.",
          "worked": "Choose a vertical line $x=c$ with $c\\ne0$. Its intersection with the curve must satisfy $y=\\frac1{c^2}$, so there is exactly one intersection point, $(c,\\frac1{c^2})$. For example, $x=1$ intersects the curve only at $(1,1)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      }
    ]
  },
